result: {"query":"Dignaga apoha exclusion theory Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy Buddhist philosophy of language","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_018QoNS8Etgjz4LoQjCG4S7Q","content":[{"title":"Dharmakīrti (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/dharmakiirti/"},{"title":"Śāntarakṣita (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/saantarak-sita/"},{"title":"Dignāga`s Philosophy of Language: Pramāṇasamuccayavṛtti on anyāpoha. Part I and Part II","url":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctt1hj9zx3"},{"title":"Apoha - Routledge Encyclopedia of Philosophy","url":"https://www.rep.routledge.com/articles/thematic/apoha/v-1"},{"title":"Epistemology in Classical Indian Philosophy (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology-india/"},{"title":"Apoha","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apoha"},{"title":"Pramanavarttika","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pramanavarttika"},{"title":"J%C3%B1anasrimitra","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J%C3%B1anasrimitra"},{"title":"LANGUAGE AND COGNITION: EXAMINING THE ROLE OF APOHA IN BUDDHIST THEORIES OF MEANING AND SEMANTICS","url":"https://www.granthaalayahpublication.org/Arts-Journal/ShodhKosh/article/view/5446"}]},"I found comprehensive information about Dignāga's apoha exclusion theory from the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy and other sources.

To solve the problem of how language could refer to real things, Dignāga devised the theory of concepts known as *apoha* ("exclusions"). Dignāga's response claims that language does not refer to universals but rather through a negative process of exclusions or *apoha*, eliminates that which contradicts the referred object, with words talking about entities as qualified by the negation of other things.

*Apoha* or exclusions typically involves a double negation, and because in Buddhism the absence of something is a fiction in that absences are not caused and do not function, exclusions can be used to explain away universals as the referents of words. The basic theory behind apoha is that meaning comes from what something is not—for example, "cow" gets its meaning by excluding every non-cow.

Dharmakīrti reinterpreted this theory in terms of causal relations, and each of the Buddhist logico-epistemological tradition (*pramāṇavāda*) thinkers began to address these questions with variations on a theory initiated by Dignāga and fine-tuned by Dharmakīrti and his followers, known as exclusion (*apoha*) theory."],"durationSeconds":6.563885666999966,"searchCount":1}

