input: {"url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/algebra-logic-tradition/","prompt":"What does it say about Tarski's 1941 'On the calculus of relations', relation algebra, Peirce and Schroeder as predecessors, composition (relative product), and Tarski-Givant? Short paraphrase."}
result: {"bytes":79209,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Tarski's 1941 Relation Algebra Work

Tarski's 1941 paper revived the algebra of relations by treating it as an equationally defined class with multiple models beyond standard ones. He built on Peirce's earlier work, which Schröder had systematically developed in his three-volume *Algebra der Logik*, particularly regarding composition (the relative product of relations).

Tarski's key contribution was showing that "quantifier-free relation formulas [could] be reduced to equations," making relation algebra an equational theory. This paralleled how Boolean algebra related to set theory.

Later, in *Set Theory Without Variables* (1987), Tarski and Givant demonstrated that relation algebra's expressive power was sufficient to formalize first-order set theory—a remarkable result showing the tradition's continued mathematical significance decades after the algebraic logic approach had fallen from favor.","durationMs":3501,"url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/algebra-logic-tradition/"}

