Partee, Barbara Hall published "Some structural analogies between tenses and pronouns in English" in 1973 in The Journal of Philosophy 70(18): 601–609.

Partee motivated the pronominal analysis of tense via a series of interpretive parallels between tenses and pronouns, arguing that tenses, like pronouns, can have their reference determined contextually or deictically, can corefer with other elements of the same type, and can be bound by an antecedent or a quantifier.

Regarding the connection to temporal reference frameworks mentioned in your query, the search results show that English tenses are anaphoric to an aforementioned reference point, which is sometimes construed as a time (e.g. Reichenbach 1947, Partee 1973, Stone 1997) and sometimes as an event (e.g. Kamp 1979, 1981, Webber 1988). This indicates connections between Partee's work and the approaches developed by researchers like Kamp (who is cited along with Reyle in temporal semantics frameworks).

The search results confirm that Partee's 1973 work on structural analogies between tenses and pronouns is a foundational text in how anaphoric reference time is understood in contemporary semantic theories."],"durationSeconds":6.2631242079995575,"searchCount":1}

