result: {"query":"Rubin 1915 figure-ground contour belongs to figure thing-character ground extends behind Wagemans century of Gestalt psychology","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01AU1QBagLpd5qNaXgbkP9Gn","content":[{"title":"A model of figure ground organization incorporating local and global cues","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2003.06731"},{"title":"Gestalt principles - Scholarpedia","url":"http://www.scholarpedia.org/article/Gestalt_principles"},{"title":"Rubin vase","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubin_vase"},{"title":"Chapter Six Figure and Ground We see things because light from","url":"https://utoronto.scholaris.ca/bitstreams/8831729b-9342-4cbb-b4ce-5e0b6be73f38/download"},{"title":"Figure-Ground (Perception) - an overview","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/veterinary-science-and-veterinary-medicine/figure-ground-perception"},{"title":"From Local Cues to Global Percepts: Emergent Gestalt Organization in Self-Supervised Vision Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.00718"},{"title":"Local and global gestalt laws: A neurally based spectral approach","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1512.06566"},{"title":"Locating and Editing Figure-Ground Organization in Vision Transformers","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.06407"},{"title":"Lower Region: A New Cue for Figure–Ground Assignment","url":"http://awhvogellab.com/files/pdfs/vecera_2002_lower-region-new-cue.pdf"}]},"Based on the search results, I can provide information about Rubin's 1915 work on figure-ground organization and how it relates to Gestalt psychology and Wagemans' century review:

Rubin presented in his doctoral thesis (1915) a detailed description of the visual figure-ground relationship, and one fundamental principle from his research is: "When two fields have a common border, and one is seen as figure and the other as ground, the immediate perceptual experience is characterized by a shaping effect which emerges from the common border of the fields and which operates only on one field or operates more strongly on one than on the other".

Regarding the contour and how it belongs to the figure, the bounding contour will be seen as belonging to the figure shape, which appears interposed against a formless background.

This type of field organization was first described in the work of Rubin (1915/1921), predating Wertheimer's publication. Rubin's figure–ground distinction influenced the Gestalt psychologists, who discovered many similar percepts themselves.

