result: {"bytes":102858,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Sherrington and the Synapse

According to the article, Sherrington introduced the term "synapse" in 1897 when revising nervous system chapters for a physiology textbook. He used this concept to describe how information travels between nerve cells through contact points.

The article indicates that synaptic transmission was understood as a unidirectional process, building on Santiago Ramón y Cajal's work showing nerve currents moved one way through the brain and spinal cord. At synapses, sensory and motor elements connected through contacts between neurons in the spinal cord's grey matter.

The text emphasizes Sherrington's focus on how excitatory and inhibitory processes interact at these synaptic connections to control motor neuron output. Later research using microelectrodes confirmed his predictions that excitation and inhibition produced opposite electrical changes in nerve cell membranes—depolarization versus hyperpolarization—operating like "algebraic summation of opposites."","durationMs":3505,"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1079269"}

