input: {"url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-judgment/","prompt":"Kant's hypothetical judgment (ground and consequence) in the table of judgments; and time as form of inner sense. What does it say?"}
result: {"bytes":131088,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Kant's Hypothetical Judgment and Time as Inner Sense

Based on the Stanford Encyclopedia entry, Kant's table of judgments categorizes hypothetical judgments under the "Relation" category. According to the text, hypothetical judgments are "molecular" propositions that combine simpler subject-predicate statements through conditional structure.

The entry states that hypothetical judgments take the form: **"If Fs are Gs, then Hs are Is"** (or simply "If P then Q"). These represent one of three relational forms, alongside categorical and disjunctive judgments.

Regarding time as the form of inner sense, the document identifies it as one of the "necessary and non-empirical forms of empirical intuition." Time, along with space, constitutes what Kant calls our subjective forms of sensory intuition—the a priori structures through which we organize all inner experience.

The text notes that these forms "ultimately express the outer and inner sensory aspects of the embodiment of our minds." In Kant's framework, time structure applies to representations of our own mental states and processes (inner sense), just as space structures our perception of external objects (outer sense).","durationMs":3826,"url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kant-judgment/"}

