input: {"url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/chinese-logic-language/","prompt":"Any discussion of Xunzi's theory of names, general/specific names (gong/bie), or conventionality? Short paraphrase."}
result: {"bytes":143733,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Xunzi's Theory of Names: General and Specific Categories

According to the webpage, Xunzi developed a Confucian appropriation of Mohist logic that emphasized naming conventions. The text notes that Xunzi "marshals highly intelligible theories of semantics and reference in their defense, and he explicates these theories with punctilious definitions that are marked improvements over earlier formulations."

Regarding general versus specific names, the webpage indicates Xunzi recognized that "names of different scope" existed—some names classify multiple things while others refer to individual objects. This distinction mirrors the Mohist framework of categorizing names by their referential scope.

Critically, Xunzi's approach prioritized **conventionality and authority** in naming practices. Rather than grounding correctness in abstract principles, he deferred to "the more fastidious judgments of an intellectual elite" to establish proper naming conventions. His theory maintained that correct naming serves normative functions—clarifying social hierarchies and guiding ethical behavior—but ultimately relied on established cultural authority and ritual propriety (_li_) to justify these conventions, distinguishing his Confucian position from the Mohists' appeal to objective criteria.","durationMs":4035,"url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/chinese-logic-language/"}

