Xu Shen's stated purpose in his postface was "to establish defined categories, [to] correct mistaken concepts, for the benefit of scholars and true interpretation of the spirit of language." More specifically, the lexicographic structure of the *Shuowen* was designed to prove that changes in writing systems are historically and graphemically observable, so that the original intentions of the sages who used *guwen* (ancient script forms) to write the classics are literally recoverable.

Regarding the preservation of original graph forms, Xu Shen needed to base his graphological dictionary on the small seal script and not on the current clerical script of his time, which helps illustrate why preserving these earlier forms was essential to his project.

Xu Shen used his "six scripts" (*liùshū*) theory to explain character formation, categorizing characters based on their composition. This theoretical framework became foundational to Chinese character analysis.

The search results provide support for the connection between Xu Shen's work and the preservation of ancient graph forms against contemporary script variation, though the specific sources you mentioned (Boltz's "Early Chinese Writing" or the Cambridge History of Ancient China) did not appear in these particular results."],"durationSeconds":6.2802789169996975,"searchCount":1}

