"Alles wirkliche Leben ist Begegnung" appears in one of the most important writings of religious philosopher Martin Buber. This line is often cited from Buber's work and translates to "All real living is meeting" in English. The German phrase "Alles wirkliche Leben ist Begegnung" has multiple English translations: "All real life is meeting" or "All actual life is encounter."

Buber began working on *Ich und Du* midway through the First World War and published the final version in 1923. The work presents a "dialogical" philosophy distinguishing two fundamental word-pairs—"I–Thou" and "I–It"—where in the I–Thou relation, persons encounter one another as whole beings in mutual presence, while in the I–It relation, beings are experienced, used, and objectified.

